/// Key service (`keystore`, issue 103, MC-11.9): holds the device key (Ed25519, made once from RDRAND and kept in the
/// service's private directory of the boot disk, 351-NET-0005; never exported) and its self-signed client certificate. No call returns the private key. Signing is an
/// operation with a purpose and a budget: only the signer's badge (the TLS service) may ask for it, the data must have
/// the form the purpose names, and every signature and refusal goes to the system log. Anyone may read the certificate.
///
/// 1.1 (550-NET-0024): the WPA2-PSK networks the Wi-Fi station remembers. Their PMKs are kept here, in the private
/// directory, wrapped under a key derived from the device key (so sealed with it where a TPM seals it); no call returns
/// one. Only the station's badge (`BADGE_KEY_STATION`, `mind::network`) may call these, and it gets each handshake's
/// keys, within a budget per minute, each logged.
///
/// 1.2 (351-NET-0027): SSH logins. The purpose `ssh-login` signs only what a public-key login with the device key
/// signs, only for the SSH service's badge (`BADGE_KEY_SSH`, `mind::network`), within its own budget per boot; and
/// anyone may read the device's public key (`public-key`), as the certificate.
///
/// 1.3 (550-NET-0035): the Wi-Fi station's country, kept in the private directory beside its networks: a two-letter
/// code, never a row of a catalogue. Only the station's badge reads or changes it; the station changes it only on a
/// change client's word (wifi.wit 1.1 `set-country`).
///
/// 1.4 (000-NET-0045): a program's secrets, a token, a password or an API key that its program alone reads. The loader
/// asks for a client for each program it runs by its name (`secrets-client`, `secrets-name`, `secrets-adopt`: its badge
/// `BADGE_KEY_LOADER` alone) and lends it in SLOT_SECRETS (000-KRN-0182). That client is answered only for the task the
/// loader named, or the first that calls on it, and only about its program's secrets: no call names another program.
/// They are kept in the private directory, one file per program, sealed (ChaCha20-Poly1305) under a key derived from
/// the device key and the program's name; the log names a program and a secret, never a value.
///
/// 1.5 (308-KRN-0183): a boot service's secrets. init lends `blockstore` a client badged `BADGE_KEY_BLOCKSTORE`
/// (`mind::network`), on which the secrets calls are about that service's own secrets: its volume keys (308-STO-0022).
/// No program reaches them, whatever its name: they are kept apart, one file per service in `system/keystore/services/`,
/// sealed under a key whose label names a service, so a program's file copied there opens no more than under another name.
///
/// 1.6 (308-NET-0055): the device key sealed under the owner's passphrase where no TPM seals it. Until the owner sets
/// one, the key stays as in 1.0, unsealed in the private directory; once set (`passphrase-set`), it is sealed under it
/// in `system/keystore/device.locked` (Argon2id of the passphrase, then ChaCha20-Poly1305), and every boot waits for it:
/// `key-state` says `waiting` until `unlock` opens it. Only the passphrase's badge (`BADGE_KEY_PASSPHRASE`,
/// `mind::network`) may call `unlock` and `passphrase-set`; the log names each refusal, never a passphrase.
///
/// 1.7 (110-NET-0069): the TLS service's server role. The purpose `tls-server` signs only a TLS 1.3 server
/// CertificateVerify, for the signer's badge alone, at most 120 a minute (its own budget, apart from `tls-client`'s per
/// boot), each signature logged: the device key serves as the machine's TLS server key without leaving this service.
package mind:keystore@1.7.0;

interface keystore {
    /// Why a request failed. `missing` (1.4): no secret of that name is kept.
    enum error { denied, budget, invalid, no-key, missing }

    /// What a signature is for. `tls-client`: a TLS 1.3 client CertificateVerify (RFC 8446 4.4.3): 64 spaces, the
    /// context string "TLS 1.3, client CertificateVerify", a zero byte and a SHA-256 or SHA-384 transcript hash; for the
    /// signer's badge. `ssh-login` (1.2): what an SSH public-key login signs (RFC 4252 section 7): a 32-byte session
    /// identifier, SSH_MSG_USERAUTH_REQUEST, a user of 1 to 64 printable characters without space, "ssh-connection",
    /// "publickey", TRUE, "ssh-ed25519" and the device key's own blob, nothing after; for the SSH service's badge.
    /// `tls-server` (1.7): a TLS 1.3 server CertificateVerify, as `tls-client`'s with the context string "TLS 1.3,
    /// server CertificateVerify"; for the signer's badge, at most 120 a minute (`budget` once they are used).
    enum purpose { tls-client, ssh-login, tls-server }

    /// The TLS client signatures made, their budget for this boot, requests refused of every purpose (`ssh-login` and
    /// `tls-server` have budgets of their own).
    record usage { signatures: u32, budget: u32, refused: u32 }

    /// The device certificate (DER).
    certificate: func() -> result<bytes<512>, error>;
    /// An Ed25519 signature of `data` for `purpose` (`tls-client` and `tls-server`: the signer's badge only;
    /// `ssh-login`: the SSH service's badge only).
    sign: func(purpose: purpose, data: bytes<256>) -> result<bytes<64>, error>;
    usage: func() -> result<usage, error>;

    /// A network the station remembers (1.1): its name as the access point sends it.
    record wifi-network { ssid: list<u8, 32> }

    /// Keeps the PMK of WPA2-PSK network `ssid`, in place of one kept for the name (station badge only; `budget` when 16
    /// others are kept, `no-key` without the device key).
    wifi-remember: func(ssid: list<u8, 32>, pmk: list<u8, 32>) -> result<_, error>;
    /// The networks kept (station badge only).
    wifi-networks: func() -> result<list<wifi-network, 16>, error>;
    /// The CCMP pairwise keys of one 4-way handshake with network `ssid` (KCK, KEK and TK, 16 bytes each), from its PMK,
    /// the access point's and the station's addresses (low 48 bits, first byte in bits 40-47) and their nonces (station
    /// badge only; `invalid` when the network is not kept, `budget` past 60 a minute).
    wifi-keys: func(ssid: list<u8, 32>, authenticator: u64, supplicant: u64, anonce: list<u8, 32>, snonce: list<u8, 32>) -> result<list<u8, 48>, error>;
    /// Forgets network `ssid`; false when none was kept (station badge only).
    wifi-forget: func(ssid: list<u8, 32>) -> result<bool, error>;

    /// The device's Ed25519 public key (1.2), which an SSH server lists as an authorized key.
    public-key: func() -> result<list<u8, 32>, error>;

    /// The station's country (1.3): two capital letters, empty when none is kept (station badge only).
    wifi-country: func() -> result<string<2>, error>;
    /// Keeps the station's country (1.3), replacing the one kept; `invalid` when `code` is not two capital letters (so
    /// never `00`), `no-key` when it could not be written (station badge only).
    wifi-keep-country: func(code: string<2>) -> result<_, error>;

    /// A new client for one program's secrets (1.4, the loader's badge only), answered once `secrets-name` names its
    /// program; it ends with the task `secrets-adopt` names, or after a minute when no task took it up. `budget` when 64
    /// clients live.
    secrets-client: func() -> result<borrow<endpoint>, error>;
    /// Names the program whose secrets client `client` (its badge) reaches: 1 to 16 lowercase letters, digits, `.`, `_` or
    /// `-`, as the loader names a task. Once, before any call on it, by the loader that asked for it.
    secrets-name: func(client: u16, program: string<16>) -> result<_, error>;
    /// Makes client `client` task `task`'s alone, as if that task had called on it first (only the loader that asked for it).
    secrets-adopt: func(client: u16, task: u64) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Keeps `value` (1 to 16384 bytes) as the caller's program's secret `name` (1 to 32 letters, digits, `.`, `_` or `-`),
    /// in place of one kept under the name; `budget` past 16 secrets or 65536 bytes of them for one program, `no-key`
    /// without the device key or when it could not be written (a secrets client, or a boot service's (1.5), only).
    secret-put: func(name: string<32>, value: bytes<16384>) -> result<_, error>;
    /// The caller's program's secret `name`; `missing` when none is kept (a secrets client, or a boot service's (1.5), only).
    secret-get: func(name: string<32>) -> result<bytes<16384>, error>;
    /// Forgets the caller's program's secret `name`; false when none was kept (a secrets client, or a boot service's (1.5), only).
    secret-delete: func(name: string<32>) -> result<bool, error>;
    /// The names of the caller's program's secrets, never their values (a secrets client, or a boot service's (1.5), only).
    secret-list: func() -> result<list<string<32>, 16>, error>;

    /// Whether `keystore` holds the device key (1.6): `held`; `waiting`, sealed under a passphrase not typed yet, or
    /// typed wrong; `none`, no device key (no random source, or a sealed file it does not open). Any client may ask, and
    /// it is answered at once, except while an `unlock` or a `passphrase-set` derives its key.
    enum key-state { held, waiting, none }
    key-state: func() -> result<key-state, error>;
    /// Opens the device key sealed under `passphrase` (1.6, the passphrase's badge only; `denied` to others). `denied`
    /// when it does not open it, `budget` when it comes, from the third wrong one in a row, before the waiting time
    /// (from 1 s, doubling to 60 s), `invalid` when nothing waits (a TPM seals the key, or it is held).
    unlock: func(passphrase: string<128>) -> result<_, error>;
    /// Seals the device key held under `new`, 8 to 128 bytes, in place of the passphrase `old`, empty when none is set,
    /// then removes its unsealed file (1.6, the passphrase's badge only; `denied` to others). A passphrase once set is
    /// never removed. `denied` when `old` does not open the one set, `budget` as `unlock`'s (a wrong `old` counts with
    /// them), `invalid` with a TPM, while the key waits, or for a `new` of another length, `no-key` without the device
    /// key or when it could not be written.
    passphrase-set: func(old: string<128>, new: string<128>) -> result<_, error>;
}
